The tenth guest on OWNDAYS MEETS is Chocomoo, an illustrator known around the world for her pop-art and unique black-and-white illustrations. Having collaborated with apparel brands and captivated the imagination of a range of artists, Chocomoo tells us about the path she has taken so far as well as what the road ahead has in store for her.
I went to New York because I’ve always liked music and I wanted to experience real hip-hop and R&B, and while I was there I did lots of illustrations just for my own pleasure. I really didn’t think that I wanted to make a living out of illustration, and to be honest I still don’t really feel that I want to make it my sole focus!
Fortunately, my current job involves doing something I really love, and I just hope that I can carry on in my own style. So you really like drawing pictures!
I’m not really aware of the fact that I’m doing it as work, and when I want to refresh myself or relax I grab a pen and just start drawing. I do drawings just for myself when I’m travelling by plane or train; it really is just something I do in my everyday life.
How did you end up having your own unique style of illustration?
I’m based in Kyoto and I’ve liked Japanese traditions and styles for a long time, so having been influenced by the temples of Kyoto and the pop-art of overseas I wanted to do illustrations that integrate and combine these styles. You wouldn’t think that I actually love temples from the way I like.
Live painting is exciting because people watch me as I work, and in contrast to when I’m shut away in the studio, the finished product depends on the day so I myself also find it really exciting.
Do you have any challenges you want to take on or dreams for the future?
Also, collaborations with photographers interest me. In the past a photographer who has photographed a range of artists over the years contacted me and I ended up painting on top of some of the photos, which were then exhibited in Florida.
