← Collective Trauma in Our Communities
When people experience or witness violent acts like abuse and murder, they can experience a host of symptoms resulting from that trauma, including numbness, depression, anxiety, anger, helplessness, flashbacks, agitation, and nightmares. These are normal and understandable reactions to horrific events that should not be normal.
Mental Health America of Los Angeles is committed to furthering the health, safety, and access to resources — housing, education, health care, and employment — for all members of our community. As an agency, we are working to bring more resources to our communities to address the impact of trauma through initiatives that build resilience.
No person or group of people should have to live in fear. Until everyone is safe, treated fairly and equally, and has access to needed resources, our larger communities will not be mentally healthy.
The mental wellbeing of our entire community has been severely hurt by the killing of George Floyd by a Minnesota police officer. For our African American community members, who live with the daily fear that their children, their family members or they themselves could be killed, the pain of this situation is beyond describable.
Before the COVID-19 pandemic hit Los Angeles County, Julian attended weekly group meetings at the Transition-Age Youth Academy in Long Beach. Every Friday, he and the other TAY students would come together to enjoy food, talk about community issues, and play games.
‘The Skills I Learned Helped My Mom and I Get Jobs’
‘I Took My Weakness and Made it a Strength’
‘This Is Where My Miracle Happened’
‘My Job Brought Back the Fire in My Heart’
Angel got his MHALA start at the Transition-age Youth program in the Antelope Valley. As he began building his confidence, he learned about the Employment Services program, eventually joining the work adjustment team.
“They taught me how to apply myself to the job, how to create my resumes to appeal to employers,” Angel explains. “We also did mock interviews.
“I used the skills I learned at MHALA to help my mom apply for and interview at Amazon for the same position. And she got in as well!”
