My name is Rennie and there’s not enough time in a day to tell you of all the trauma, poor decisions, impaired judgement, all the different things that set my life on a self-destructive path from age 8 to age 47.
It’s not a curse – it’s truly a blessing. When I look back on my life I have to say, what was the turning point of my life that made me stop showering in the side of somebody’s house using their water hose?
What made me stop stealing and hurting people?
The judicial system advised me to either accept help or go to jail for the rest of your life. They said, “Either you accept that you need help or go to jail for the rest of your life.”
I was staying clean by working a 12-step program. I found a counselor who believed in me, for some reason.
She didn’t say, “Well, first you need to get your teeth fixed and you need to take baths. You need your hair combed.” She didn’t say any of that.
I went on to obtain to an AA and a BA, along with several certificates in substance abuse. At 54, I became a college graduate.
Because I’m new to life — as our members are new to life, living life on life’s terms.
I’ll never get a job. What am I going to do with this education?
“But you don’t understand.” I was going to let my history hold me back … but we can move past it. I’m a living example.
Um, I do have some education…” I’m going along with it because I had a glimmer of hope already.
When I went to court they said, “Only five people a year get a certificate of rehabilitation and you’re one of them. You’re going to be pardoned.”
For the first time in my life I can vote. Now don’t forget, we still have mental health issues … but today, I get to know my barriers.
There’s a real magical purpose here. Every time I walked through the door, I was home.
I tore boards off of abandoned houses and crawled through them and got in a corner. So coming from that place, I saw kings and queens and princesses and homeowners and business owners and I thought, it can happen.
