The word "emoji”Comes from the union of“ e ”(絵), which means image in Japanese and“ moji ”(文字), which means letter. That is, they are symbols that represent a complete idea, word or phrase.
Today they are part of writing and are even considered a new language. For example, the word of the year 2015, chosen by the Oxford dictionary, was !
Do not confuseEmojis FacebookwithEmoticons Facebook. While emojis are stand-alone characters, emoticons are emoticons or symbols made with keyboard characters, such as (-: or <3.
The Emojis Facebook they are already part of our culture and day by day it is difficult to remember when they came and where, right?
It all started when the cell phone company NTT DoCoMo developed I-mode, a type of information package for cell phones, including email, weather, news, and Internet access, which was well advanced at the time and sought to revolutionize the way that the Japanese reported.
But none of them agreed and that's when Kurita, who was not a designer, assembled a small team and, with the limitation of 12 × 12 pixels, invented the first 176 emojis that have a history.
According to Kurita, his inspiration for these early symbols comes from Japanese culture and expressions he saw in anime and manga as a child.
Shortly after the emojis were released to the general public, the other telecom companies brazenly copied the designs and idea of the characters, adding a few new additions, as the NTT company DoCoMo and Kurita did not patent the invention.
- which only started to be solved in 2005, in which all companies decided to map which characters were used and try to establish a standard between them.
Okay, but how did the "Emojis Facebook " from Japan and took over the world?
But here in the west, emojis were still hidden on the iPhone and were only discovered when users changed the language of the device to Japanese.
And that was the beginning of the fever as we know it today: Google and Windows tried to get their emojis inspired by the competition's design.
Today, emojis are part of the culture, and are used from everyday communication to storytelling, as in the case of the book Emoji Dick, which adapted the classic story of Moby Dick using only emojis.
With the great popularity of Emojis Facebook and the way they are inserted into the culture of the world, good sellers have seen the innumerable possibilities that this language can provide for communication with their customers.
As we saw in the statistics of the last topic, the92%of internet users today use emojis.
1. Know your person
Before you think about what to say and how to say it, know if your persona engages with the use of emojis. Is it that your audience is exactly in the 8% that you still do not use and do not identify with the characters?
2. Know the meanings of the Emojis Facebook
A good tip is to use Emojipedia to discover the meaning of all emojis, in addition to researching the chosen characters on social networks to see how people use them and in what context.
Someone who speaks directly to your brand can come up and be an important way to interact with your audience.
I know we press the person key a lot, but it is really important that your communication is tuned to the language of who you want to reach.
As we saw there in the early days of emojis, their creation was precisely to convey emotions that were not possible in the text and to make the messages less cold and distant.
Not everyone can easily understand what the symbols mean, so be careful with messages that are completely encoded with emojis. Your message may not get through, and you may miss out on creating a connection with your audience.
