These symbols have the appearance of expressions, objects, animals, types of weather, etc. They became a fever with the increasing use of smartphones.
But the story is much older than it seems and begins in 1999, in Japan, as the origin of the name "emoji" already denounces.
Shigetaka Kurita, one of the NTT DoCoMo employees participating in the I-mode project, realized how cold and sometimes ambiguous communication was by limiting only the phrases written on the cell phone, and began looking for a way to make the messages more human.
Their first step was to try to convince the big tech companies that were partners in the I-mode project, like Panasonic and Sharp, to draw these symbols, since most of them had equipment and resources for that.
When the creator of the emojis finally presented the original designs to the big tech companies, he hoped they would improve them with their structure, but that didn't happen: the symbols went into mode I exactly the way they were originally designed.
This ended up spawning countless different characters and real chaos - the number of emojis hit 800!
In 2012, one of NTT DoCoMo's competitors, with the help of Kurita, finally redesigned the original characters.
If you thought of Apple, you're right!
In 2007, Apple released the first iPhone and, considering its popularity in the Japanese market, included emojis, as these were a very strong cultural phenomenon in the country.
Given the desire of the public who also wanted to communicate using friendly symbols and faces, Apple launched emojis for all languages in 2011, with the launch of iOS.
To avoid the chaos that started emojis in Japan, the Unicode Consortium went to the trouble of standardizing the character code so that users of different operating systems can use the emojis without disagreement.
And it's not just with millennials!
Despite being a very strong trend, it is necessary to be careful in its use, not to exaggerate or cause misunderstandings. Therefore, we have brought some tips for you to use emojis in your business marketing:
This is the fundamental step before thinking of any communication, as we always talk about here on the blog.
For this research and testing use. See what type of communication your person reacts the most and how you adapt.
One of the biggest mistakes when using emoji in marketing is using the characters without really knowing the meaning. Not all of them can represent what you think, so it is important to know exactly what you are communicating.
You may have noticed, but the emojis are constantly updated.
Also note what your person has been using, what the trends are and which ones have no longer been used.
